{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"software as a service software as a service","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md","fileName":"Software as a Service.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Software as a Service","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 22:39\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Cloud Service and Deployment Models]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Service and Deployment Models","cloudcomputing_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"software as a service software as a service","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Software as a Service.md","fileName":"Software as a Service.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Software as a Service","heading":"Software as a Service","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Software as a service delivers a hosted application that performs a recognizable function for its users. The consumer uses the application without installing, updating, or directly managing the operating system and infrastructure beneath it.\n\nExamples include customer relationship management, billing, communication, office productivity, and rendering tools. SaaS can lower local maintenance and support needs, but availability, data protection, provider dependency, export formats, and backup remain consumer concerns.\n\nThe book connects SaaS to service design thinking: the provider owns not only executable software but also deployment, monitoring, adaptation, and the continuing outcome the customer receives as needs change.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Service and Deployment Models","cloudcomputi